    {
        "content": "<p><code>SeparationQuotient</code> is not <code>implicit_reducible</code> so the <code>Quotient.map'</code> here is defeq abuse</p>",
        "id": 626326103,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1790171651
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I had tried to add <code>attribute [local implicit_reducible] SeparationQuotient</code> in my file, to no avail. Maybe it's just too late, let me try to add it right at the definition.</p>",
        "id": 626326616,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1790171754
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, it works. Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 626328251,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1790172086
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Just a guess: If <code>implicit_reducible</code> only works if set early enough, I suspect that some <code>inferInstanceAs</code> that came before the reducibility change wrapped an instance field into an auxiliary definition. That auxiliary definition is semireducible to hide the defeq abuse. Perhaps <code>rfl</code> would need that definition to be unfolded, too, alongside with <code>SeparationQuotient</code> itself.</p>",
        "id": 626328880,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1790172209
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The underlying reason for the non-substitutive behavior is that Lean feels the need to check whether the types of the two instances' fields, which are functions, are the same (at implicit transparency). They aren't, so Lean fails. An alternative quick-and-dirty fix is to insert a <code>cast rfl &lt;|</code> or <code>id &lt;|</code> in front of the field definition so that the type of the field is exactly as expected.</p>\n<p>(Since <code>SeparationQuotient</code> has its own instances, I don't advise to just make it implicit-reducible.)</p>",
        "id": 626339407,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1790174179
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am confused. I thought that <code>implicit_reducible</code> for type synonyms with different instances was the way to go.</p>",
        "id": 626340259,
        "sender_full_name": "Sébastien Gouëzel",
        "timestamp": 1790174266
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That was my claim before <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14949\">lean4#14949</a>. We discussed a possible partial mitigation in <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"270676\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/transparency.2C.20rewriting.2C.20and.20typeclasses/with/624262406\">#lean4 &gt; transparency, rewriting, and typeclasses</a>, but the complexity it adds is significant. I'm worried about explaining this approach to newcomers, so we can't claim <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14949\">lean4#14949</a> solved at this point.<br>\nI know that we have such <code>implicit_reducible</code>s in the code base right now, and they are working as expected except for <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/lean4/pull/14949\">lean4#14949</a>, so <code>implicit_reducible</code> can be a fix for now, but I still think it would be more sustainable to solve it differently.</p>",
        "id": 626344041,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1790174871
    }
]